# closure Phase 2 Step A wiring proof: an implicit-`it` closure
# (`.map(it*2)`, lowered to __Closure_0) followed by an EXPLICIT closure
# (`(int x): x+1`, __Closure_1). Before the make-site-single-id refactor the
# two never-reconciled closure-id counters desynced on mixed modules: the
# explicit closure's `__make_closure_N` value wired to the implicit-it body
# (`it*2`) instead of its own body (`x+1`). This fixture proves the fix —
# the explicit closure's __make_closure points at its OWN __Closure__call.
#
# ⚠ IT ALSO READS THE MAPPED VALUES, and that is load-bearing. It used to read
# only `doubled.len()`, and stayed green right through a self-host miscompile
# that filled the accumulator with unmapped, uninitialized elements: the COUNT
# was right and only the values were garbage (SIX QUESTIONS #6 — a green cell
# green for a reason unrelated to what it tests). The wide net for that defect
# is `hof_implicit_it/collection_axis.gg`; this fixture carries the `map` cell
# inside the auto-scanned corpus.
void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, 3]
    Vector[int] doubled = xs.map(it * 2)
    print(doubled.len())
    for d in doubled:
        print(d)
    int(int) f = (int x): x + 1
    print(f(5))
